WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:01.966
Poupyrev:
My name is Ivan Poupyrev,

00:00:01.966 --> 00:00:04.300
and I work for Advanced
Technology and Projects group

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.800
at Google.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.966
The hand is the ultimate
input device.

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:10.800
It's extremely precise,
it's extremely fast,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.533
and it's very natural
for us to use it.

00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:15.333
Capturing the possibilities
of the human hand

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.633
was one of my passions.

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:19.766
How could we take
this incredible capability,

00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:24.666
the finesse of human actions
and finesse of using our hand,

00:00:24.666 --> 00:00:29.066
but apply it
to the virtual world?

00:00:29.066 --> 00:00:32.900
We use radio frequency spectrum,
which is radars,

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.066
to track human hand.

00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:37.227
Radars have been used
for many different things--

00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:40.666
to track cars, big objects,
satellites and planes.

00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:44.333
We're using them
to track micro motions,

00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:45.733
twitches, of the human hand

00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.400
and then use that
to interact with wearables

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.700
and Internet of Things

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.900
and other computing devices.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.033
Lien: Our team is focused
on taking radar hardware

00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:00.133
and turning it
into a gesture sensor.

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:01.766
Radar is a technology

00:01:01.766 --> 00:01:04.966
which transmits a radio wave
towards a target,

00:01:04.966 --> 00:01:06.733
and then the receiver
of the radar

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:10.566
intercepts the reflected energy
from that target.

00:01:10.566 --> 00:01:12.766
The reason why we're able
to interpret so much

00:01:12.766 --> 00:01:14.700
from this one radar signal

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.133
is because of the full
gesture recognition pipeline

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:18.600
that we've built.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.433
The various stages
of this pipeline

00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:24.166
are designed to extract
specific gesture information

00:01:24.166 --> 00:01:26.500
from this one radar signal
that we receive

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.500
at a high frame rate.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.200
Amihood: From these strange,
foreign range Doppler signals,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.533
we are actually interpreting
human intent.

00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:39.800
Karagozler: Radar
has some unique properties

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.500
when compared to cameras,
for example.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.733
It has very high
positional accuracy,

00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:50.833
which means that you can sense
the tiniest motions.

00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:52.900
Schwesig: We arrived
at this idea of virtual tools

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.400
because we recognized
that there are

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.800
certain archetypes of controls,
like a volume knob

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.633
or a physical slider,
a volume slider,

00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:03.100
Imagine a button

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.733
between your thumb
and your index finger,

00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:07.566
and the button's not there,

00:02:07.566 --> 00:02:10.166
but pressing this
is a very clear action.

00:02:10.166 --> 00:02:12.366
And there's an actual
physical haptic feedback

00:02:12.366 --> 00:02:14.500
that occurs as you perform
that action.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.733
The hand can both embody
a virtual tool,

00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:19.466
and it can also be, you know,

00:02:19.466 --> 00:02:21.666
acting on that virtual tool
at the same time.

00:02:21.666 --> 00:02:23.700
So if we can recognize
that action,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.833
we have an interesting direction
for interacting with technology.

00:02:28.833 --> 00:02:31.033
Poupyrev: So when we started
this project,

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:35.466
you know, me and my team,
we looked at the project idea,

00:02:35.466 --> 00:02:37.433
and we thought,
"Are we gonna make it or not?

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:38.900
Eh, we don't know."

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.266
But we have to do it.

00:02:40.266 --> 00:02:43.200
Because unless you do it,
you don't know.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.833
Raja: What I think I'm most
proud of about our project is,

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:49.100
we have pushed
the processing power

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.500
of the electronics itself
further out

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.866
to do the sensing part for us.

00:02:55.866 --> 00:02:57.133
Poupyrev:
The radar has a property

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:58.833
which no other technology has.

00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:00.466
It can work through materials.

00:03:00.466 --> 00:03:02.066
You can embed it into objects.

00:03:02.066 --> 00:03:04.033
It allows us to track
really precise motions.

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:05.833
And what is most exciting
about it

00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:08.300
is that you can shrink
the entire radar

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.000
and put it in a tiny chip.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.800
That's what makes this approach
so promising.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.333
It's extremely reliable.

00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:15.600
There's nothing to break.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.733
There's no moving parts.

00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:17.800
There's no lenses.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.566
There's nothing,
just a piece of sand

00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:22.266
on your board.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.560
Schwesig: Now we are at a point
where we have the hardware

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.853
where we can sense
these interactions,

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.047
and we can put them to work.

00:03:30.047 --> 00:03:31.907
We can explore
how well they work

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.686
and how well they might work
in products.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.200
Poupyrev:
It blows your mind, usually,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.960
when you see things people do.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.073
And that I'm really
looking forward to.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:44.940
I'm really looking forward

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.620
to releasing this
to the development community,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.174
and I really want them
to be excited

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.200
and motivated

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.420
to do something cool with it,
right?

